<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amalasuntha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 495–<a href="/years/535">535</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Amalaswintha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amalasuntha was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> and ruled the Ostrogothic Kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as regent for her son Athalaric from 526. Educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek, she favoured friendship with the Byzantine court, which angered Gothic nobles. After her son's death she made her cousin Theodahad co-ruler, but he had her imprisoned and killed in 535, giving Justinian a pretext for war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amalasuntha.jpg" alt="Amalasuntha" width="320" height="552" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_femminile_di_tipo_ariadne-amalasunta,_480-525_dc_ca,_da_s.m._ai_monti_(musei_capitolini).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Theodoric's death in 526, the murder of his daughter <b>Amalasuntha</b> gave Justinian a pretext to invade in 535.</blockquote><a href="/state/ostrogothic-kingdom">Ostrogothic Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ravenna, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Isola Martana, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.42,12.2&amp;year=521" aria-label="Ravenna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4842 593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Amalasuntha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5292 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5292 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.42,12.2&amp;year=521">What was here in 521 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is in books that the sage counsellor finds deeper wisdom, in books that the warrior learns how he may be strengthened by the courage of the soul, in books that the Sovereign discovers how he may weld nations together under his equal rule.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Senate, 534 (Cassiodorus, Variae, book 10, letter 3) &#183; tr. Thomas Hodgkin, 1886 &#183; a letter written in her name by Cassiodorus, praising her cousin Theodahad on making him co-ruler &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18590/pg18590-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The stars give one another mutual help in ruling the heavens, and God has bestowed on man two hands, two ears, two eyes, that each one of these members should assist the other.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Senate, 534 (Cassiodorus, Variae, book 10, letter 3) &#183; tr. Thomas Hodgkin, 1886 &#183; a letter written in her name by Cassiodorus, on sharing her throne &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18590/pg18590-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
